  "response": "몸은 자고 싶은데 머리가 계속 돌아가는 그 상태, 정말 지치셨을 것 같아요. 낮에 나눴던 얘기들이 떠오른다고 하셨는데, 그 얘기들이 보통 어떤 종류예요? 갈등이 있었던 상황이라든가, 뭔가 제대로 말하지 못한 것 같은 느낌 같은 건가요?",
  "reasoning": "The client is describing their sleep problem: when they lie down, conversations from the day keep replaying in their mind, their eyes are tired but their brain won't shut off, sometimes lasting until 2-3 AM, and their body is lying down but tension doesn't release.\n\nThis is a clear description of rumination at bedtime with physical tension. The client has given me enough detail to work with. I should:\n\n1. Acknowledge what they've shared - validate the experience without being overly sympathetic\n2. Maybe gently explore what kind of conversations or themes keep coming back, since \"conversations from the day\" is somewhat vague - but I need to be careful not to interrogate\n3. Or I could reflect back the experience of the disconnect between body wanting rest and mind being active, which seems to be the core frustration\n4. I could also gently explore whether this is connected to something specific or if it's a general pattern\n\nI think the most helpful response here would be to reflect the experience of that tension between body and mind, and perhaps gently explore what the recurring conversations are about - whether they're about conflict, worry, things they wish they'd said differently, etc. This could help identify whether there's an underlying anxiety or unresolved interpersonal concern driving the rumination.\n\nI'll keep it concise and natural, acknowledging the experience and asking one focused question about what the conversations tend to be about.\n",
